.TH ATTACH 5
.SH NAME
attach, auth \- messages to establish a connection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B size[4] Tauth tag[2] afid[4] uname[s] aname[s] n_uname[4]
.B size[4] Rauth tag[2] aqid[13]
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B size[4] Tattach tag[2] fid[4] afid[4] uname[s] aname[s] n_uname[4]
.B size[4] Rattach tag[2] qid[13]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tattach
is a client's introduction to a server.
It names a user,
.IR uname ,
and may select which of several trees to serve,
.IR aname ,
though every server in the tree answers the same one regardless of
what
.I aname
names.
As a result of the transaction the client holds
.I fid
on the root of that tree, and
.I qid
is the server's identity for it.
An error is returned if
.I fid
is already in use.
.PP
.I N_uname
is 9P2000.L's numeric uid, used in place of
.I uname
when it is not
.BR NONUNAME .
Nothing in the tree distinguishes the two: there is one user and no
login, and every server accepts whatever
.I uname
a client sends.
.PP
.I Afid
names a fid established by a prior
.B Tauth
exchange, or
.B NOFID
when the client offers no authentication.
No server in the tree implements
.BR Tauth :
.I attach
always sends
.BR NOFID ,
and a server given anything else is free to refuse it.
There is no authentication server yet, and every attach succeeds or
fails on the strength of the name space alone.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/vectra9/proto.odin
.br
.B kernel/vfs/walk.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (5),
.IR version (5),
.IR walk (5),
.IR vectra9 (2)
